Make a session on the local display the console's login.

On LikeOS the kernel names the user logged in at the console: the leader of
the session whose controlling terminal is the console, when it is a
recorded login (/proc/self/loginuid) of the user it runs as.  That user is
granted the seat's devices (the GPU render node), has drives mounted for
it by the device manager and may power the machine off or restart it.  A
console login gets all of that from getty and login; a graphical one did
not, so a desktop started by LightDM ran without the GPU and could not shut
the machine down, and neither could the greeter.

So the forked session process -- the greeter's as well as the user's, on
the local display only -- records the login and takes the console as its
controlling terminal, as root, after setsid() and before it becomes the
user.  The login has to be recorded there and not only by PAM: the kernel
ends the login a privileged process belongs to when it starts a session,
so what pam_open_session() recorded for the session helper does not
survive the setsid().  Only the forked process runs this, with only
async-signal-safe calls.

